 # Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction
 
-Also called: EVolSplat, Efficient Volume-based Gaussian Splatting, GRM, Gaussian MLP Heads, Gaussian Parameter Prediction, Gaussian Representation Model
+Also called: EVolSplat, Efficient Volume-based Gaussian Splatting, GRM, Gaussian MLP Heads, Gaussian Representation Model
 
 ## Overview
-Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction refers to feed-forward methods that directly predict the geometric attributes (position, opacity, covariance) of 3D Gaussians from input data, bypassing the iterative per-scene optimization required by standard 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1, S2]. Unlike sibling initialization methods that set static initial values for subsequent gradient-based refinement, this approach outputs the final or near-final parameters in a single pass [S2]. It distinguishes itself from pixel-aligned methods (e.g., MVSplat) by operating on a global volumetric representation, which improves global consistency and reduces ghosting artifacts caused by redundant primitives in overlapping regions [S2].
+Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction refers to feed-forward methods that directly predict the geometric attributes (position, covariance, opacity) of 3D Gaussian primitives from input images or point clouds, bypassing the iterative per-scene optimization required by standard 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1, S2]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on heuristic densification or local cost volumes, this approach typically utilizes a global volumetric representation or learned priors to ensure multi-view consistency and reduce ghosting artifacts in overlapping regions [S2]. It enables real-time novel view synthesis and 3D reconstruction from sparse or uncalibrated inputs by mapping input data to a complete Gaussian set in a single pass [S1, S2].
 
 ## How it works
-The method typically follows a pipeline of global point cloud construction, feature volume extraction, and MLP-based parameter decoding, often incorporating recursive refinement for position offsets [S2].
-
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** A set of $N$ posed input images $\{I_n\}_{n=1}^N$, their intrinsic matrices $\{K_n\}$, extrinsic matrices $\{T_n = R_n | t_n\}$, and optionally monocular depth estimates $\{D_n\}$ [S2].
-*   **Outputs:** A set of $N_p$ 3D Gaussians $\mathcal{G} = \{(\mu_i, \alpha_i, \Sigma_i, c_i)\}_{i=1}^{N_p}$, where $\mu_i$ is the center, $\alpha_i$ is the opacity, $\Sigma_i$ is the covariance matrix, and $c_i$ is the view-dependent color [S2].
+The method varies by specific implementation (e.g., EVolSplat vs. BrightDreamer), but the canonical volume-based approach (EVolSplat) operates as follows:
