 ### Defocus Blur Modeling
-Defocus Blur Modeling adjusts the scale $s$ of 3D Gaussians based on their distance from the camera's focal plane [S3]. During training, larger scale values $\bar{s}_j$ are used to rasterize blurred images, while during testing, smaller scale values $s_j$ are used to render sharp details [S3]. Unlike a naive Gaussian kernel that blurs the entire image uniformly, this method selectively enlarges covariances only for Gaussians corresponding to blurred regions [S3].
+Defocus Blur Modeling adjusts the scale $s$ of 3D Gaussians based on their distance from the camera's focal plane to model optical defocus [S3]. During training, larger scale values $\bar{s}_j$ are used to rasterize blurred images. During testing, smaller scale values $s_j$ are used to render sharp details [S3]. The scale $s$ controls dispersion; larger $s$ models out-of-focus points, smaller $s$ models in-focus points [S3]. Unlike a naive Gaussian kernel that blurs the entire image uniformly, this method selectively enlarges covariances only for Gaussians corresponding to blurred regions [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Feed-forward inference is significantly faster than iterative optimization, as it avoids per-scene gradient descent. The complexity is dominated by the 3D CNN feature extraction and MLP decoding, which scales with the number of points $N_p$ and the volume resolution [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the global point cloud $\mathcal{P}$ and the feature volume $\mathcal{F}$. The sparse 3D CNN reduces memory usage compared to dense volumes, but it is still higher than pixel-aligned methods that do not maintain a global 3D structure [S2].
-*   **Performance:** EVolSplat achieves real-time rendering by feed-forward prediction [S2]. It improves global consistency and reduces ghosting artifacts compared to pixel-aligned methods like MVSplat, Flash3D, and DrivingForward [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles large-scale urban scenes efficiently; reduces ghosting artifacts through global volume processing; leverages 3D context for better geometry prediction [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the accuracy of monocular depth estimators; may lose high-frequency appearance details if primitives are downsampled too aggressively (addressed by IBR-based color prediction) [S2].
-*   **When to use:** Suitable for urban scene reconstruction and applications requiring fast, feed-forward 3DGS generation from sparse views [S2].
-*   **When not to use:** Not suitable for scenes where high-precision per-pixel alignment is critical without global consistency, or where iterative optimization is acceptable for higher fidelity [S2].
