 ## Background
-Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction emerged as a solution to the slow, per-scene optimization required by standard 3D Gaussian Splatting [S2]. The method was introduced in EVolSplat (2025) by Miao et al., which proposed an efficient volume-based approach for urban view synthesis [S2]. It builds on the idea of using global volumetric representations to improve consistency, contrasting with earlier feed-forward methods that predicted per-pixel Gaussians using local cost volumes or epipolar transformers [S2]. The approach utilizes existing multi-view generation models and sparse 3D CNNs to train a pixel-aligned (but globally consistent) Gaussian representation [S1].
+Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction emerged as a solution to the slow, per-scene optimization required by standard 3D Gaussian Splatting [S2]. Early feed-forward methods predicted per-pixel aligned Gaussians using local cost volumes or epipolar transformers, which suffered from multi-view inconsistencies and duplicated content [S2]. EVolSplat introduced a volume-based approach that predicts 3D Gaussians across multiple frames within a unified global volume using a 3D convolutional network, improving global consistency and reducing ghosting artifacts [S2]. Other variants like BrightDreamer focus on text-to-3D generation by predicting deviations from anchor points [S1]. The method is widely used in urban view synthesis, autonomous driving applications, and 3D generation tasks [S1, S2].
 
